#edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537957 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "affed96b-3c3d-54a9-a279-f8432cb1a012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537957 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that we can write this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538009 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186538039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538009 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a8560ad-d3a4-5e23-a9c1-ad08b689d611" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538009 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186250172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538023 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186538060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538023 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "567dadd3-ca44-5bf9-8b98-d51